## What Problem This Solves

`readSandboxPolicyFile` in the MXC extension used unbounded `readFileSync` to load user-supplied sandbox policy JSON files, allowing a very large policy file to exhaust memory during policy loading.

## Why This Change Was Made

The policy file read now uses `readRegularFileSync` from `openclaw/plugin-sdk/security-runtime` capped at 1 MiB, matching the bounded-read pattern already adopted by `qqbot`, `tts-local-cli`, and `whatsapp` extensions. Oversized policy files are rejected before JSON parsing. Symlink rejection is intentional hardening — sandbox policy paths are explicit operator configuration, not symlink-based indirection — and documented in a JSDoc block on the cap constant. Error messages for oversized files avoid duplicating the file path that `readRegularFileSync` already includes.

## User Impact

Operators configuring custom MXC sandbox policies are protected against accidental OOM from oversized policy files. Normal policy files are far below 1 MiB and are unaffected.

## Evidence

### Real behavior proof (production module, public API)

```text
$ pnpm exec tsx proof-live.ts
boundaryBytes=1048576
boundaryTimeoutSeconds=60
oversizedRejected=true
missingRejected=true
```

The temporary proof script exercises `loadSandboxBaselinePolicy` (the changed module's public API) with real filesystem I/O. A valid policy padded to exactly the 1 MiB compatibility boundary loads successfully; a 1 MiB + 1 byte file is rejected by the bounded-read helper before parsing; and the existing missing-file behavior remains intact. The script was removed after the run and is embedded below for reproducibility.

### Tests

```
$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/mxc/test/sandbox-policy-loader.test.ts

 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  2 passed | 8 skipped (10)
   Start at  21:19:18
   Duration  542ms
```

2 cross-platform bounded-read tests pass (normal < 1 MiB loads, > 1 MiB rejected). 8 additional Windows-only tests pass on their target platform.

<details>
<summary>proof test source</summary>

```ts
import { mkdtempSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { loadSandboxBaselinePolicy } from "./extensions/mxc/src/sandbox-policy-loader.js";

const limit = 1024 * 1024;
const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "openclaw-mxc-proof-"));

try {
  const boundaryPath = join(dir, "boundary.json");
  const oversizedPath = join(dir, "oversized.json");
  const missingPath = join(dir, "missing.json");
  const policyJson = JSON.stringify({ process: { timeoutSeconds: 60 } });

  writeFileSync(boundaryPath, policyJson + " ".repeat(limit - Buffer.byteLength(policyJson)));
  const policy = loadSandboxBaselinePolicy({ policyPaths: [boundaryPath] });
  console.log(`boundaryBytes=${limit}`);
  console.log(`boundaryTimeoutSeconds=${policy.process?.timeoutSeconds}`);

  writeFileSync(oversizedPath, "x".repeat(limit + 1));
  try {
    loadSandboxBaselinePolicy({ policyPaths: [oversizedPath] });
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`oversizedRejected=${String(error).includes("File exceeds 1048576 bytes")}`);
  }

  try {
    loadSandboxBaselinePolicy({ policyPaths: [missingPath] });
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`missingRejected=${String(error).includes("does not exist")}`);
  }
} finally {
  rmSync(dir, { force: true, recursive: true });
}
```
</details>

### Code quality improvements over v1

- Cap constant (`MX_SANDBOX_POLICY_MAX_BYTES`) moved adjacent to its single call site with a JSDoc block explaining the 1 MiB ceiling and intentional symlink rejection.
- Error deduplication: `readRegularFileSync` already includes the file path in its rejection message; the wrapper no longer prepends the path a second time.
- Bounded-read behavior covered by cross-platform tests in the existing test file.

