## What Problem This Solves

`tasks cancel` on a live Codex-native subagent only cleared the task-registry row without interrupting the active turn. The parent agent remained blocked in wait_agent until stuck-session recovery aborted it (~7 min). The root cause is in task-registry.ts:2385 where the `!childSessionKey` branch (from #83185) did not distinguish stale registry cleanup from live thread cancellation.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Codex app-server exposes `turn/interrupt` (JSON-RPC) to cancel an active turn on a child thread. The original code never called this method, so a live Codex thread kept running after cancel. This change wires the cancel path through to the Codex app-server using the documented protocol, bridging core↔extension via Symbol.for() to respect the ownership boundary without expanding the plugin SDK surface.

**Lifecycle safety**: the interrupt request sets a pending-state flag (`interruptRequested`) instead of immediately finalizing the mirrored task. The authoritative terminal delivery happens only when the app-server's `turn/completed(interrupted)` notification arrives. All three notification paths — live `handleChildTurnCompletion`, reconciliation `reconcileChildState`, and task-row recovery `reconcileCodexNativeTask` — check `interruptRequested` and deliver the cancelled completion from the authoritative event.

Compatibility: stored task-registry rows with `codex-thread:*` runIds now try interrupt before falling through to registry-only cleanup. Stale rows (no live monitor) retain unchanged behavior. No config, env, or schema change. Performance: one Symbol.for() lookup per cancellation plus one RPC call; no runtime polling added. Usability: one working cancel path. Security: no auth or secret surface change.

## User Impact

- `tasks cancel` on a live Codex-native subagent now interrupts the active turn within seconds instead of blocking the parent for ~7 minutes
- The mirrored task is finalised only after the app-server confirms the turn was interrupted
- Stale codex-native tasks (no live monitor) still fall through to registry-only cleanup, preserving existing behavior
- Natural turn completion or error before the interrupt takes effect is handled correctly (the terminal event from `turn/completed` with "completed"/"failed" status takes precedence; `interruptChildThread` reads `childState.terminal` and returns false)
- No config, env, or schema changes

## Evidence

